The Willows hedgehog Center in  Bromsgrove like dozens of others across  the UK is full the mild weather is  largely to blame for the problem it's  meant many hedgehogs have had litters  later in the year too late for the  youngsters or hoglets to put on enough  weight to survive hibernation we've been  going nine years over nine years and  this is the first time we have ever  closed our doors for new admissions  we've never had to do it before it's  only through the kindness of foster  carers like Sam that spaces can be freed  up to allow new admissions well I've  always been really fascinated by  hedgehogs and I saw the advert on  Facebook here couple years ago so I just  rang in had to chat to Jane came and  tried it out really enjoyed it and  obviously because she gets so many  hedgehogs come in particularly at this  time of year I thought it'd be really  helpful to her if I could help out with  the fostering so I did and it's just  gone from there the latest figures from  the RSPCA show the number of hedgehogs  admitted to its wildlife centres is up  twenty-two percent so far this year from  January to the end of October more than  2000 hedgehogs were brought in that's  compared to 1739 over the same period  last year  an average of twelve hedgehogs were  taken into the centres every day in  October  that's roughly one every two hours of  course there are guidelines to be  followed if you're considering fostering  a hedgehog or a hoglet as cute as he may  be he's certainly not a pet the advice  is that they shouldn't be kept inside a  house if you want to look after one you  need a garage or a shed and it's not  yours to keep he is going to be released  back into the wild  the Hedgehog is one of Britain's  favourite wild animals but popularity is  no guarantee of protection we're well  aware that Hedgehog numbers in this  country are in serious decline people  are aware of the plight of the Hedgehog  so when they're seeing these youngsters  when they're seeing these small  hedgehogs that are going to be too small  to survive hibernation they're acting we  will take him  there's his reference number so if you  want an update send us a text message  and when we  got time we will respond so the advice  is if you see a small hedgehog at any  time or a larger one during the day you  need to help wearing gloves pick it up  weigh it if you can and contact your  local rescue center that's what Emma did  and that's why this lucky little chap  will now be able to survive the winter  Fraser Maud skynews Bromsgrove
 

